    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justinian-i.jpg" alt="Justinian I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosaic_of_Justinianus_I_-_Basilica_San_Vitale_(Ravenna).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Petar Milošević, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> were a confederation of steppe horsemen who appeared on the edge of the Byzantine world in 558, when their envoys reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> asking for land and payments. Their origins are debated. Byzantine writers claimed they were fugitives from the Turks, and many historians link them with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rouran-khaganate">Rouran Khaganate of Mongolia</a>, destroyed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Göktürks</a> in the 550s; ancient DNA studies published in 2022 indicate that the early Avar elite had recent roots in northeastern Asia, which supports a connection of this kind. Moving west across the Pontic steppe, they subdued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a> and other groups and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a>, capturing the Frankish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigibert-the-lame">Sigibert</a> I in a campaign of the 560s. Their decisive opportunity came in 567, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> Bayan I allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alboin">Alboin</a> to destroy the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a> in the eastern Carpathian basin. In 568 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombards">Lombards</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and the Avars took over the whole basin, the great plain enclosed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>. From there Bayan built a power that dominated the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> for more than two centuries and claimed the title of khagan, equal in rank to the rulers of the Turks.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gepids"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gepids.jpg" alt="Gepids" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Europe_and_the_Near_East_at_476_AD.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Guriezous, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The khaganate was a multi-ethnic society. At its top stood a mounted warrior elite of steppe origin; beneath it lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gepids">Gepids</a>, Romanised provincials, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutrigurs">Kutrigurs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a> and, above all, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>, who formed much of the population and provided farmers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxilia">auxiliary troops</a>. In the early period the elite lived largely on plunder and on tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, paid in gold coin: the annual payment rose from 80,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solidus">solidi</a> in the 570s to 100,000 and later 120,000 under the emperor Maurice. Byzantine coins and gold objects are common in rich early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> graves. From the later 7th century, as tribute dried up, Avar society became more settled, with villages, mixed farming and stock raising. Tens of thousands of graves excavated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> document the culture of this period, especially the cast bronze belt fittings decorated with griffins and tendril patterns that distinguish the Late Avar era. Belt sets marked rank and status. Avar religion was probably shamanistic and remained largely pagan until the Frankish conquest, though individual Christian objects appear in graves. Little is known of their language, which may have been Turkic or Mongolic, and they left no written records of their own, so their history is reconstructed from Byzantine and Frankish texts and archaeology.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/priscus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/priscus.jpg" alt="Priscus" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PriscusofPanium.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> military power rested on heavily armed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horse-archer">horse archers</a> who used the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/composite-bow">composite bow</a>, lances and armour for both men and horses, supported by large Slav infantry contingents. The Byzantine military manual known as Maurice's <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategikon-of-maurice">Strategikon</a></em>, written around 600, describes Avar tactics and recommends that Roman cavalry copy their equipment, and the Avars are generally credited with bringing the iron stirrup into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Under Bayan I they besieged and took the Byzantine fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a> in 582 after a long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, then captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Singidunum</a> and raided deep into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>. Maurice's generals, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/priscus">Priscus</a>, campaigned north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the 590s and defeated the Avars near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viminacium">Viminacium</a> and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a> in 599, but the emperor's overthrow in 602 ended the counter-offensive. The climax came in 626, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a>, in coordination with a Persian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahrbaraz">Shahrbaraz</a> on the Asian shore, besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> with Avar, Slav and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> forces. The land walls held, and the Slav boat fleet was destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horn">Golden Horn</a>. Later campaigns were mostly defensive. Earthwork rings, of which the main royal centre was known to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> as the Ring, protected the khagan's treasure, but they could not stop the Frankish armies of the 790s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar.jpg" alt="Chronicle of Fredegar" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Chronicle of Fredegar</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paris,_BnF_MS_Latin_10910_fol._23v_Chronicle_of_Fredegar_-_Miniature_Eusebius,_Jerome_and_a_mallard_drake.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> stood at the head of the state as a sacral ruler descended from a charismatic dynasty, receiving tribute and envoys and distributing treasure to his followers. Byzantine and Frankish sources mention other high officials with steppe titles, among them the <em>jugurrus</em>, the <em>tudun</em> and the <em>kapkhan</em>, whose exact functions are uncertain. Authority was personal and depended on the khagan's success in war and in redistributing wealth; when that success faltered, subject peoples broke away. The Slav subjects were governed indirectly through their own leaders, obliged to provide troops and supplies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-fredegar">Fredegar</a> records the harshness of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> lordship over them. The seat of power lay in the central plain, probably between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisza">Tisza</a>, although the exact location of the khagan's Ring has never been identified. The Avars conducted active diplomacy, exchanging embassies with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, and the khagan Bayan negotiated forcefully over the size of the annual tribute. After the failure of 626 a crisis followed: around 631 a struggle for the khaganate broke out between an Avar and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgar</a> candidate, and the defeated Bulgars fled west to Frankish territory. The khaganate survived, but its rule became more regional, focused on the Carpathian basin.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle.jpg" alt="Primary Chronicle" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:14_2_List_of_Radzivill_Chron.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">from the Middle Ages, unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For half a century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> were the leading power between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. Their raids and the settlement of their Slav allies helped transform the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>: Byzantine control of the interior collapsed in the late 6th and early 7th centuries, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> settled widely south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, a process the Avar wars accelerated. The revolt of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samo's Slavs</a> around 623 and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Great Bulgaria</a> in the 630s were both reactions against Avar lordship, and so were linked to the formation of new states. The khaganate shaped Byzantine military practice through the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategikon-of-maurice">Strategikon</a></em> and through the long Danube wars of Maurice. In central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> its fall opened the way for the Frankish and Bavarian expansion into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and for the Christian missions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquileia">Aquileia</a> among the Slavs, which in turn prepared the ground for Great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>. Late Avar metalwork is among the richest bodies of early medieval art from the region, and the many excavated cemeteries make the Avars one of the archaeologically best-known peoples of the period. The Avars also lived on in memory: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Russian Primary Chronicle</a> describes the <em>Obri</em> as great in body and proud in mind, and records a proverb that they perished without trace.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charlemagne.jpg" alt="Charlemagne" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlemagne_denier_Mayence_812_814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charlemagne. Autorité émettrice de monnaie indéterminé. Atelier monétaire. Émetteur, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Avar Khaganate fell to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. After Frankish annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 788, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avar</a> attacks on Bavaria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friuli">Friuli</a> failed, and in 791 Charlemagne led a great campaign down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raba">Raab</a>. Internal conflict then weakened the Avars: in 795 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khagan">khagan</a> and the <em>jugurrus</em> were killed in a civil war, and the <em>tudun</em> offered his submission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>. In the same year Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-friuli">Eric of Friuli</a> sent a force that plundered the Ring, and in 796 Charlemagne's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy">Pippin of Italy</a> destroyed it and carried its treasure to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aachen">Aachen</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einhard">Einhard</a> wrote that no war had brought the Franks richer spoils. Avar leaders accepted baptism and Frankish overlordship, and rebellions in the following years were crushed. In 805 a Christian khagan, Theodore, asked Charlemagne for land between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savaria">Savaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a> because of attacks by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. The eastern Avar lands were probably taken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krum">Khan Krum</a> early in the 9th century. Avars are last mentioned in 822, when their envoys appeared at an assembly of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>. The remaining population merged with Slavs and others, and the Carpathian basin later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magyars">Hungarians</a> after 895.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Avar Khaganate ruled the Carpathian basin for two and a half centuries, extracting gold from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 626 and driving the Slav settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and its destruction by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> in the 790s reshaped central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> to Frankish, Bulgarian and later Hungarian power.</em></p>

